<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> <appendix
xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ap-cardinality">
  <title>Cardinality</title> <section xml:id="sec-cardinality-subsets-real">
    <title>Cardinality</title> <p>
      For sets <m>A,B</m>, <m>|A|=|B|</m> means there is a bijection between
      them. In that case, we say that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same
      <term>cardinality</term><idx>cardinality</idx>. We write <m>|A|
      \le |B|</m> if there is an injection <m>A \to B</m>.  A set <m>A</m>
      is <term>countably infinite</term><idx>countably infinite</idx> if
      <m>|A|=|\N|</m>. A set is <term>countable</term><idx>countable</idx>
      if it is either finite or countably infinite. In other words, a set
      <m>A</m> is countable if and only if there is a surjection from
      <m>\N</m> to <m>A</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We begin with standard countable models that will be reused in later proofs.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-n-plus-zero-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\N\cup\{0\}</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>

      <p>
          The map <m>f:\N\to \N\cup\{0\}</m>, <m>f(n)=n-1</m>, is bijective,
          with inverse <m>g(m)=m+1</m>.  Hence <m>|\N\cup\{0\}|=|\N|</m>.
        </p>

	</proof>
    </proposition>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-n-times-two-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\N\times\{0,1\}</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </statement> <proof>
        <p>
          Define <m>F:\N\times\{0,1\}\to\N</m> by <m>F(n,0)=2n</m>
          and <m>F(n,1)=2n-1</m>.  This is bijective, so
          <m>|\N\times\{0,1\}|=|\N|</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition> <proposition xml:id="prop-n-square-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\N^2</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </statement> <proof>
        <p>
	  For each <m>s\in\N</m>, the diagonal
	  <m>D_s=\{(m,n)\in\N^2:m+n=s\}</m> is finite.	Also
	  <m>\N^2=\bigcup_{s\ge2}D_s</m> is the union of a countable
	  family of countable sets and hence countable by <xref
	  ref="prop-countable-union-countable"/>.
	</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition> <p>
      The next theorem is the main comparison tool.
    </p> <theorem xml:id="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder">
      <title>Cantor-Bernstein-Schroder</title> <statement>
	<p>
	  If <m>|A|\le |B|</m> and <m>|B|\le |A|</m>, then <m>|A|=|B|</m>.
	</p>
      </statement>
    </theorem> <p>
      Proofs of <xref ref="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder"/> can be found in
      <xref ref="biblio-garrett-csb"/> and <xref ref="biblio-wikipedia-csb"/>.
    </p> <proposition xml:id="prop-countable-union-countable">
      <statement>
	<p>
	  A countable union of countable sets is countable.
	</p>
      </statement> <proof>
	<p>
	  Let <m>A_1,A_2,\ldots</m> be a countable family (indexed by
	  <m>\N</m>) of countable sets.  For <m>a\in\bigcup_{j\in\N}A_j</m>,
	  let <m>j(a)</m> be the least index with <m>a\in A_{j(a)}</m>.
	  Then <m>a\mapsto (a,j(a))</m> is an injection into
	  <m>\bigcup_j(A_j\times\{j\})</m>.  For each <m>j</m>, choose an
	  injection <m>f_j:A_j\to\N</m>.  Then <m>(a,j)\mapsto (f_j(a),j)</m>
	  injects <m>\bigcup_j(A_j\times\{j\})</m> into <m>\N^2</m>.
	  Since <m>\N^2</m> is countable, the union is countable.
	</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition> <proposition xml:id="prop-q-countable">
      <statement>
	<p>
	  The set <m>\Q</m> is countable (hence <m>\Z</m> is countable).
	</p>
      </statement> <proof>
	<p>
	  The map <m>n\mapsto n/1</m> gives an injection <m>\N\to\Q</m>, so
	  <m>|\N|\le |\Q|</m>.	For the reverse inequality, map <m>r\in\Q</m>
	  to <m>(m,n)\in\Z\times\N</m> where <m>r=m/n</m> in lowest terms and
	  <m>n&gt;0</m>.  This is injective, and <m>\Z\times\N</m> is countable
	  (as a countable union of countable sets), so <m>|\Q|\le |\N|</m>.
	  By <xref ref="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder"/>, <m>|\Q|=|\N|</m>.
	</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition> <p>
	  Let <m>X</m> be a set and let <m>A\subseteq X</m>.  The
	  <term>indicator function</term><idx>indicator function</idx>
	  of <m>A</m> in <m>X</m>, denoted <m>1_A^X</m> (or <m>1_A</m>
	  when <m>X</m> is understood), is the map <m>1_A^X:X\to\{0,1\}</m>
	  defined by <m>
	    1_A^X(x)=
            \begin{cases}
              1,&amp;\text{if }x\in A,\\ 0,&amp;\text{if }x\notin A.
            \end{cases}
          </m></p>
    <p>
      For any set <m>X</m>, indicator functions give a bijection
      <m>\mathcal{P}(X)\cong 2^X</m>, where <m>2^X</m> is the set of maps <m>X\to\{0,1\}</m>.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-cantor-power-set">
      <title>Cantor</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For every set <m>X</m>, <m>|X| &lt; |2^X|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The map <m>x\mapsto 1_{\{x\}}</m> is an injection <m>X\to2^X</m>.  To show there is no
          surjection <m>X\to2^X</m>, suppose <m>\Phi:X\to2^X</m> is surjective.  Define
          <m>d:X\to\{0,1\}</m> by <m>d(x)=1-\Phi(x)(x)</m>.  Since <m>\Phi</m> is surjective,
          <m>d=\Phi(a)</m> for some <m>a\in X</m>.  Evaluating at <m>a</m> gives
          <m>\Phi(a)(a)=1-\Phi(a)(a)</m>, impossible.  Hence <m>|X|&lt;|2^X|</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-r-uncountable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The real line <m>\R</m> is uncountable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="thm-cantor-power-set"/>,
          <m>|\N|&lt;|2^{\N}|</m>.  Define <m>\Psi:2^{\N}\to\R</m> by
          <m>\Psi((a_k)_{k\ge1})=\sum_{k\ge1}a_k/10^k</m>.  If <m>a\ne
          b</m>, let <m>n</m> be the first index with <m>a_n\ne b_n</m>.
          Then <m>|\Psi(a)-\Psi(b)|\ge 10^{-n}(1-1/9)&gt;0</m>, so <m>\Psi</m>
          is injective.  Therefore <m>|2^{\N}|\le |\R|</m>, and thus <m>\R</m>
          cannot be countable.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-irrationals-uncountable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The irrational numbers <m>\R\setminus\Q</m> are uncountable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>\R\setminus\Q</m> were countable, then
          <m>\R=(\R\setminus\Q)\cup\Q</m> would be a union of two countable
          sets, hence countable, contradicting <xref ref="cor-r-uncountable"/>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </section>


  <exercises xml:id="sec-cardinality-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-n-square-explicit-bijection">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>\N^2</m> is countable by constructing an explicit
          bijection from <m>\N</m> to <m>\N^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>T_r=r(r+1)/2</m> for <m>r\geq 0</m>. For
          <m>k\in\N</m>, set
          <md>
            r=r(k)=\left\lceil\frac{\sqrt{8k+1}-1}{2}\right\rceil
            \quad\text{and}\quad
            j=j(k)=k-T_{r-1}.
          </md>
          Then <m>T_{r-1}&lt;k\leq T_r</m>, so <m>1\leq j\leq r</m>.
          Define <m>F:\N\to\N^2</m> by
          <md>
            F(k)=(r-j+1,j).
          </md>
          Thus, <m>F</m> counts the lattice points one diagonal at a time:
          <md>
            (1,1),(2,1),(1,2),(3,1),(2,2),(1,3),\ldots.
          </md>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-n-square-diagonal-counting">
          <caption>The diagonal counting route. The label <m>k</m> at a
          lattice point indicates the point <m>F(k)</m>.</caption>
          <image width="58%">

	  <shortdescription>Positive lattice points labeled from 1 through
	  15 along successive diagonals, with arrows showing the counting
	  route.</shortdescription>

	    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05, &gt;=stealth]
  \draw[step=1, gray!28, very thin] (0.75,0.75) grid (5.25,5.25);
  \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0.6,0.65) -- (5.55,0.65) node[right] {$m$};
  \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0.65,0.6) -- (0.65,5.55) node[above] {$n$};
  \foreach \x in {1,...,5}
    \node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0.65) {$\x$};
  \foreach \y in {1,...,5}
    \node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] at (0.65,\y) {$\y$};
  \foreach \xa/\ya/\xb/\yb in {
    1/1/2/1, 2/1/1/2,
    1/2/3/1, 3/1/2/2, 2/2/1/3,
    1/3/4/1, 4/1/3/2, 3/2/2/3, 2/3/1/4,
    1/4/5/1, 5/1/4/2, 4/2/3/3, 3/3/2/4, 2/4/1/5}
    \draw[-&gt;, blue!70!black, thick, shorten &lt;=5pt, shorten &gt;=5pt]
      (\xa,\ya) -- (\xb,\yb);
  \foreach \x/\y/\k in {
    1/1/1,
    2/1/2, 1/2/3,
    3/1/4, 2/2/5, 1/3/6,
    4/1/7, 3/2/8, 2/3/9, 1/4/10,
    5/1/11, 4/2/12, 3/3/13, 2/4/14, 1/5/15}
    \node[circle, draw=blue!70!black, fill=white, inner sep=1.4pt,
      font=\scriptsize] at (\x,\y) {$\k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          To verify that <m>F</m> is bijective, define
          <m>G:\N^2\to\N</m> by
          <md>
            G(m,n)=T_{m+n-2}+n.
          </md>
          If <m>F(k)=(m,n)</m>, then <m>m+n-1=r</m> and <m>n=j</m>;
          hence <m>G(F(k))=T_{r-1}+j=k</m>. Conversely, given
          <m>(m,n)\in\N^2</m>, set <m>r=m+n-1</m> and
          <m>k=G(m,n)</m>. Since <m>1\leq n\leq r</m>, we have
          <m>T_{r-1}&lt;k\leq T_r</m>, and therefore
          <m>F(k)=(r-n+1,n)=(m,n)</m>. Thus <m>F</m> and <m>G</m> are
          inverses, so <m>F</m> is a bijection and <m>\N^2</m> is
          countably infinite.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-subset-of-countable-set">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that any subset of a countable set is countable.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-open-unit-interval-real-bijection">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>\R</m> have the same cardinality by constructing an explicit bijection.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-intersection-two-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that the intersection of two intervals is empty, a singleton, or an interval.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-union-overlapping-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that the union of two overlapping intervals is an interval.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</appendix>
